acy†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite57†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L162: \nL163: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2512.16145v1] MRG-R1: Reinforcement Learning for Clinically Aligned Medical Report Generation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.16145v1)\nciteturn6504view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.16145v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 18 Dec 2025 (this version), latest version 27 Mar 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:MRG-R1: Reinforcement Learning for Clinically Aligned Medical Report Generation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Pengyu Wang , cite7†Shuchang Ye , cite8†Usman Naseem , cite9†Jinman Kim L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled MRG-R1: Reinforcement Learning for Clinically Aligned Medical Report Generation, by Pengyu Wang and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Medical report generation (MRG) aims to automatically derive radiology-style reports from medical images to aid in clinical decision-making. However, existing methods often generate text that mimics the linguistic style of radiologists but fails to guarantee clinical correctness, because they are trained on token-level objectives which focus on word-choice and sentence structure rather than actual medical accuracy.\nL17: We propose a semantic-driven reinforcement learning (SRL) method for medical report generation, adopted on a large vision-language model (LVLM). SRL adopts Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to encourage clinical-correctness-guided learning beyond imitation of language style.\nL18: Specifically, we optimise a report-level reward: a margin-based cosine similarity (MCCS) computed between key radiological findings extracted from generated and reference reports, thereby directly aligning clinical-label agreement and improving semantic correctness. A lightweight reasoning format constraint further guides the model to generate structured \"thinking report\" outputs.\nL19: We evaluate Medical Report Generation with Sematic-driven Reinforment Learning (MRG-R1), on two datasets: IU X-Ray and MIMIC-CXR using clinical efficacy (CE) metrics. MRG-R1 achieves state-of-the-art performance with CE-F1 51.88 on IU X-Ray and 40.39 on MIMIC-CXR. We found that the label-semantic reinforcement is better than conventional token-level supervision.\nL20: These results indicate that optimizing a clinically grounded, report-level reward rather than token overlap,meaningfully improves clinical correctness. This work is a prior to explore semantic-reinforcement in supervising medical correctness in medical Large vision-language model(Med-LVLM) training.\nL21: Comments:  | 12 pages\nL22: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2512.16145 [cs.CL]\nL24:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2512.16145v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL25:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.16145†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Pengyu Wang [cite15†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Thu, 18 Dec 2025 03:57:55 UTC (3,874 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v2] Fri, 27 Mar 2026 07:21:26 UTC (5,039 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled MRG-R1: Reinforcement Learning for Clinically Aligned Medical Report Generation, by Pengyu Wang and 3 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite10†View PDF L39:   * cite11†HTML (exper